---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/portfolio.css';
---

<Base
  title="KODEX-∞ — Creative Technology Case Study · OCÍN"
  description="KODEX-∞ case study by Nicolás Ortega / Ocín: visual systems, worldbuilding, interaction and AI-assisted creative technology."
  robots="noindex, nofollow"
  noNav={true}
  noFooter={true}
  noOverlays={true}
  noQuote={true}
  noThemes={true}
  noKai={true}
  noCart={true}
  noImmersive={true}
>
  <div class="pf">
    <header class="pf-nav">
      <div class="pf-shell pf-nav__inner">
        <a class="pf-wordmark" href="/portfolio">OCÍN <small>Portfolio</small></a>
        <nav class="pf-nav__links" aria-label="Portfolio">
          <a href="/portfolio">Work</a>
          <a href="/kodex">Live KODEX</a>
          <a href="mailto:ocinkey8@gmail.com">Contact</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="pf-case-hero">
      <div class="pf-shell">
        <div class="pf-case-hero__grid" data-pf-reveal>
          <div>
            <p class="pf-kicker">Case 01 / Experimental system</p>
            <h1>KODEX-∞</h1>
          </div>
          <p class="pf-case-hero__lede">
            A modular visual and interactive system exploring how speculative worldbuilding,
            generative design, responsive interfaces and procedural behavior can operate
            as one coherent creative language.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pf-case-meta" data-pf-reveal>
          <div><span class="pf-meta">Role</span><strong>Creator / Creative Technologist / Art Director</strong></div>
          <div><span class="pf-meta">Status</span><strong>Active independent project</strong></div>
          <div><span class="pf-meta">Focus</span><strong>Visual systems + interaction</strong></div>
          <div><span class="pf-meta">Mode</span><strong>Experimental / iterative</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="pf-feature" data-pf-reveal>
      <img src="/img/kodex/ui/kodex-black-sun-os.png" alt="KODEX-∞ interface system study" />
    </div>

    <section class="pf-shell pf-case-block">
      <div data-pf-reveal><p class="pf-label">Challenge</p><h2>From image-making to a system that can behave.</h2></div>
      <div class="pf-case-block__body" data-pf-reveal>
        <p>
          The difficult part of KODEX was not making unusual images. It was creating enough rules that
          different scenes, interfaces and generated outputs could still feel like the same world.
        </p>
        <p>
          The project became a question of system design: what remains constant when visual style,
          motion, interaction and content change?
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pf-shell pf-case-block">
      <div data-pf-reveal><p class="pf-label">Approach</p><h2>Concept first. Grammar second. Prototype third.</h2></div>
      <div class="pf-case-block__body" data-pf-reveal>
        <p>
          I structured KODEX as seven scenes — THRESHOLD, PROLOGUE, DESCENT, ARCHIVE, MACHINE,
          COSMOLOGY and RETURN — then defined reusable rules for how typography, symbols, motion,
          interaction and narrative progression behave across them.
        </p>
        <ul class="pf-list">
          <li>Seven-scene experience architecture</li>
          <li>Visual tokens, glyph families and composition rules</li>
          <li>Motion and interaction principles rather than decorative animation</li>
          <li>Responsive and reduced-motion considerations</li>
          <li>AI-assisted visual exploration under human-defined constraints</li>
          <li>Documentation intended to make the system reusable instead of purely intuitive</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pf-section">
      <div class="pf-shell">
        <div class="pf-section__head" data-pf-reveal>
          <p class="pf-label">System evidence</p>
          <div>
            <h2>Seven scenes. One grammar.</h2>
            <p class="pf-section__intro">
              These are not presented as seven finished products. They are states in an evolving
              system, used to test whether the same visual language can survive different narrative functions.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-media-grid">
          <figure data-pf-reveal><img src="/img/kodex/brand/kodex-00-threshold.png" alt="KODEX threshold scene" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <figure data-pf-reveal><img src="/img/kodex/brand/kodex-01-prologue.png" alt="KODEX prologue scene" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <figure data-pf-reveal><img src="/img/kodex/brand/kodex-02-descent.png" alt="KODEX descent scene" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <figure data-pf-reveal><img src="/img/kodex/brand/kodex-03-archive.png" alt="KODEX archive scene" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <figure data-pf-reveal><img src="/img/kodex/brand/kodex-04-machine.png" alt="KODEX machine scene" loading="lazy" /></figure>
          <figure data-pf-reveal><img src="/img/kodex/brand/kodex-05-cosmology.png" alt="KODEX cosmology scene" loading="lazy" /></figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pf-shell pf-case-block">
      <div data-pf-reveal><p class="pf-label">My role</p><h2>Direction, systems and prototype logic.</h2></div>
      <div class="pf-case-block__body" data-pf-reveal>
        <p>
          I originate the concept and visual direction, define the scene architecture and system rules,
          direct AI-assisted exploration, evaluate outputs against the grammar and guide prototype behavior.
        </p>
        <p>
          I do not present every technical implementation as hand-written engineering. Where tools or models
          assist code or image production, the portfolio distinguishes authored direction, implementation,
          selection and integration.
        </p>
        <ul class="pf-list">
          <li>Concept development + worldbuilding</li>
          <li>Creative direction + art direction</li>
          <li>Visual-system architecture</li>
          <li>Interaction-design direction</li>
          <li>Rapid prototyping and iteration</li>
          <li>AI-assisted creative workflows</li>
          <li>Documentation + coherence review</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pf-shell pf-case-block">
      <div data-pf-reveal><p class="pf-label">What it proves</p><h2>Ambiguity can become structure.</h2></div>
      <div class="pf-case-block__body" data-pf-reveal>
        <p>
          KODEX is the clearest evidence of how I work when the problem is open-ended: define a central
          idea, identify the rules that make it recognizable, prototype how those rules behave and keep
          refining until the system can be explained to someone else.
        </p>
        <p>
          The next technical layer is deliberately smaller: public Canvas, Three.js/WebGL and AI/API
          experiments that isolate specific behaviors and make my implementation depth easier to evaluate.
        </p>
        <div class="pf-actions">
          <a class="pf-btn pf-btn--primary" href="/kodex">Open live KODEX</a>
          <a class="pf-btn" href="https://www.behance.net/gallery/239877895/KODEX-Arquitecturas-Tecno-Tribales" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Behance project</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="pf-shell pf-next">
      <a href="/portfolio">← Portfolio</a>
      <a href="/portfolio/wenu-mapu">Next: Wënü Mäpü →</a>
    </div>

    <footer class="pf-shell pf-footer">
      <span class="pf-meta">Nicolás Ortega / Ocín</span>
      <span class="pf-meta">Creative Technologist</span>
    </footer>
  </div>

  <script>
    (() => {
      const items = document.querySelectorAll('[data-pf-reveal]');
      if (!('IntersectionObserver' in window)) {
        items.forEach((el) => el.classList.add('is-visible'));
        return;
      }
      const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
        for (const entry of entries) {
          if (entry.isIntersecting) {
            entry.target.classList.add('is-visible');
            observer.unobserve(entry.target);
          }
        }
      }, { threshold: 0.08, rootMargin: '0px 0px -48px 0px' });
      items.forEach((el) => observer.observe(el));
    })();
  </script>
</Base>
